= Essential Requirements in an Arbitration Agreement
#set align(left)
#set par(justify: true)

- Whether the arbitration agreement is for an ad hoc arbitration or for institutional arbitration, it is important that it be in writing

- The model LCIA rules for example require parties to fill in the following details:
  - Number of arbitrators
  - Seat of arbitration
  - Language of the arbitration proceeding
  - Governing law of the contract
- The phrase _"disputes arising out of or in connection with the contract"_ means both issues that arise out of the contract and tort issues which are related to the contract and are arbitrable.


== Choice of Arbitrators
- The choice regarding the number of arbitrators is an important decision.
- Various considerations weigh on the parties when deciding the number of arbitrators which could adjudicate their dispute.
- A principal consideration is price, but even beyond that, the complexity of the underlying transaction is important. If the transaction is complex, it is recommended to have more than one arbitrator to appreciate and come to a reasonable resolution of the dispute.

- Qualification of arbitrators is also important though it is recommended to not specify so many categories that it becomes impossible to find arbitrators who meet all the qualifications

== Place of Arbitration

- Choosing the place of arbitration involves choosing lex arbitri.

- Parties generally want an arbitration-friendly regime where the intervention of the judiciary is limited.
- Parties may also want to choose a neutral forum that is not connected to either parties
- The situs must be within a country that is a party to the New York Convention to ensure that it is enforceable under the Convention in the jurisdiction where the assets of the parties are located
- Additionally, the parties must also look at whether the infrastructure is developed enough to sustain arbitration proceedings

== Language of Arbitration

- The language of the arbitration should be stated in the arbitration clause since in some cases the clients would have to assume unexpected costs of translating documents and witness testimony if the language of the arbitration is not specified and the tribunal takes its _own suo motu_ decision on it.

== Substantive Law

- It is the law that governs the matrix contract and is not necessarily needed to be included in the arbitration agreement, but it is always good to have it to ensure that the tribunal is clarified beyond controversy of the substantive law applicable

== Additional Provisions

- Parties could also incorporate provisions of the rules developed by the International Bar Association. Such as IBA Rules on Taking of Evidence, or Conflict of Interest.

- In case of an ad hoc arbitration that does not have provision for interim preliminary or emergency relief, parties may also want to include clauses that enable the tribunal to provide such time-sensitive relief.
- Parties could also provide for expert opinion to be included while adjudicating the dispute. For this, something around something like the Prague Rules could be incorporated for establishing the manner in which expert opinion is taken and relied upon
- Multi-tier dispute resolution clauses could be incorporated to ensure that parties are able to effectively resolve disputes at the mediation or conciliation stage without having to go through full-fledged arbitration proceedings.
- Parties may also include dispositive motions, which are motions that will summarily dispose a claim without need for a hearing.
- The manner in which legal fees and costs are to be divided between the parties is also essential. Transferring the burden of the procedure on one party alone affects the efficacy of the arbitration clause.
- Confidentiality is the hallmark of arbitration. The manner in which witness statements, expert statements and other documents presented to the tribunal are to be used, and disposed, or remained confidential should be specified.
- In a dispute that may involve more than two parties, certain considerations need to be addressed in the arbitration clause. If there are related contracts and identical arbitration clause in all contracts could consolidate the dispute process. In a singular contract, there are more than two entities which are party to the contract. Normally, one clause could suffice. However, individual parties may engage subcontractors which need to be joined in the arbitration procedure. The subcontractor is not party to the main agreement, but the arbitration clause must require any party that enters into a contract with one of the parties to the con to the agreement to be bound by the dispute resolution provisions of that agreement.
